Sculpt and rig the **Ironward Siege Tank**, a heavy tracked tank whose turret
swivels to aim a long forward gun — as a 40x30x80 opaque-voxel model using only the
`voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting discrete cells** — one recorded
operation at a time — and you **author its turret animation** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Ironward is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the tank into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape
every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a tracked, turret-swiveling
tank needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation
at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced
another way (writing files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and
flagged; only the recorded operations and the rig count.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call only records the operation to that part's log and renders
**nothing** — run `voxel-anim render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled
scene (`--component <part>` redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>`
renders the model posed at an instant of an animation), and you **must** run
`voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the
run is scored from — an unrendered part is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animation with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every operation targets a
**part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is
sculpted separately in its own preview and log.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name hull
voxel-anim fill-box --part hull --x0 6 --y0 0 --z0 5 --x1 33 --y1 16 --z1 74 --color "#5d6b3a"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig and animation subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole tank composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the turret sits centered on the hull and the gun meets the
turret's front. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates,
position each part where it belongs on the finished tank; the scene simply composes
them there.

